{"metric":"de_sitter","source":[{"metric":"de_sitter","system":"flat_slicing","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"20588b62deb9a983"},{"metric":"de_sitter","system":"static_spherical","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"43a1dad759a8ad3b"}],"views":[{"id":"static","label":"Static spherical","box":[-0.35,3.4916,-1.8708,1.8708],"system":"static_spherical","caption":["This is the whole of de Sitter spacetime, the hyperboloid $-X_0^2 + X_1^2 + \\dots + X_4^2 = L^2$ with $L = \\sqrt{3/\\Lambda}$, and each point of the diagram stands for a sphere. Its global coordinates give the metric $\\frac{L^2}{\\cos^2 T}(-dT^2 + d\\chi^2 + \\sin^2\\chi\\,d\\Omega^2)$ on the square $|T| < \\pi/2$, $0 \\le \\chi \\le \\pi$, with $X = \\chi$ across.","The static coordinates enter the square as $\\tan p = \\tanh(u/2L)$ and $\\tan q = \\tanh(v/2L)$, with $u, v = ct \\mp L\\,\\mathrm{artanh}(r/L)$, and cover the triangle about the observer at $\\chi = 0$; their horizon $r = L$ is the pair of null lines through the centre of the square. Infinity is spacelike, past and future, so every observer has an event horizon: nothing beyond the line from $(\\chi, T) = (0, \\pi/2)$ to $(\\pi, -\\pi/2)$ ever reaches the observer at $\\chi = 0$."],"layers":[{"kind":"fill","class":"region","points":[[0.0,-1.5708],[3.1416,-1.5708],[3.1416,1.5708],[0.0,1.5708]]},{"kind":"fill","class":"cover","points":[[0.0,-1.5708],[1.5708,0.0],[0.0,1.5708]]},{"kind":"line","class":"scri","points":[[0.0,1.5708],[3.1416,1.5708]]},{"kind":"line","class":"scri","points":[[0.0,-1.5708],[3.1416,-1.5708]]},{"kind":"line","class":"centre","points":[[0.0,-1.5708],[0.0,1.5708]]},{"kind":"line","class":"centre","points":[[3.1416,-1.5708],[3.1416,1.5708]]},{"kind":"line","class":"horizon","points":[[0.0,-1.5708],[3.1416,1.5708]]},{"kind":"line","class":"horizon","points":[[0.0,1.5708],[3.1416,-1.5708]]},{"kind":